There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form 

$$
A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b} .
$$

For example, let's solve the following equation:

$$
\underbrace{ {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ - 1 & 2\end{array} \right]}}_{A}

\cdot

\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
}_{\mathbf x}
=
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
}_{\mathbf b}
$$

Since the LU factorization of $A$ is

$$
A = 
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{L}
	\cdot
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{U},
$$
our equation $A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}$ can be written as

$$
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{L}
	\cdot
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{U}
\cdot
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}

=

{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
$$

i.e., 

$$
LU\mathbf{x}= \mathbf{b}.
$$

Now, suppose we set $U\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have

$$
L\mathbf{y}= \mathbf{b}.
$$

In other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\ Ux &=y\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Since $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.

- First, we solve $L\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that $\mathbf{y}={\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]}.$ Then, we have
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
=
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
$$
which gives the system of equations

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=0.\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Solving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]} = {\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ - 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

- Finally, we solve $U\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{y}.$ We have
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
=
 {\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ - 1\end{array} \right]}
$$
which gives the system of equations

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-1 \\ -{x}_{2} &=-1.\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Solving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]} = {\left[ \begin{array}{c}2 \\ 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

Find $A^{100},$ where 
