Suppose $A$ is a real $2\!\times\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:

- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\mathbf x\in\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.

- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:

$$
\mathbf x = P\mathbf u \qquad \Longleftrightarrow\qquad  \mathbf u = P^{-1}\mathbf x
$$

Therefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\mathbf x$ to $\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.

![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)

For example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by

$$
A = {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\sqrt{3} + 1 & - 2 \\ 1 & \sqrt{3} - 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

This is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. 

Using the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is

$$
A
=
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{array} \right]}
}_{P}
\cdot 
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\sqrt{3} & - 1 \\ 1 & \sqrt{3}\end{array} \right]}
}_{C}
\cdot
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{array} \right]}^{-1}
}_{P^{-1}}.
$$
The matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:
$$
A
=
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{array} \right]}
\cdot
\underbrace{\left( {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\displaystyle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}} & - {\displaystyle \frac{1}{2}} \\ {\displaystyle \frac{1}{2}} & {\displaystyle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}}\end{array} \right]} \right)}_{C}
\cdot
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{array} \right]}^{-1}
$$

Thus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:

- a rotation of $$
\displaystyle \arccos\left(\dfrac{\sqrt 3}{2}\right) = \dfrac\pi 6
$$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and

- an enlargement of scale factor ${\color{blue}{2}}.$

The image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:

![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 3 \\ 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 3\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {q}_{11}} & {q}_{12} \\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{r}_{11} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{12}} \\ 0 & {r}_{22}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

Consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of $q_{11} \cdot r_{12}.$

Let $\mathbf{a}_1,\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$

First, we find an orthonormal basis of $\textrm{Col}(A)=\textrm{Span}\{\mathbf{a}_1,\mathbf{a}_2\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.

**Step 1**. We set $\mathbf{v}_1 = \mathbf{a}_1 = {\left[ \begin{array}{c}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{array} \right]}.$

**Step 2**. We find the vector $\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\text{Span}\{\mathbf{v}_1\}$ using the formula

$$
\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\  & ={a}_{2}-\frac{{a}_{2}\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 3 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}-\frac{12}{6}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 3 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}-2\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we normalize $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_2\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\ {q}_{2} & =\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we compute $R\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\,A \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} & \frac{2}{\sqrt{6}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} & -\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 3\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\sqrt{6} & 2\sqrt{6} \\ 0 & \sqrt{3}\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\mathbin{:}$

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 3 \\ 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 3\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}}} & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}} \\ {\displaystyle \frac{2}{\sqrt{6}}} & - {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}} \\ {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}} & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\sqrt{6} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{2 \sqrt{6}} \\ 0 & \sqrt{3}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

Finally, $$
\displaystyle q_{11} \cdot r_{12} = \left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\right) \cdot \left({2\sqrt6}\right) = 2.
$$

First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}\mathbin{:}$
$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}- 2 & - 1 & 0 \\ 1 & - 2 & 1 \\ 0 & - 1 & - 2 \\ 1 & 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
}^{A}
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2} \\ {x}_{3}\end{array} \right]}
}^{\mathbf{x}}
=
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}12 \\ 0 \\ - 12 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
}^{\mathbf{b}}
$$

We can find the least-squares solution ${\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\,A\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\end{aligned}
$$

Since the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\  & ={(\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\ 1 & 0 & -1\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\,\,\,\,\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}12 \\ 0 \\ -12 \\ 0\end{bmatrix} \\  & ={\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 6\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\ 0 \\ 24\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{6}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\ 0 \\ 24\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}\mathbin{:}$
$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\ 1 & 0 \\ 2 & - 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{array} \right]}
}^{A}
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
}^{\mathbf{x}}
=
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
}^{\mathbf{b}}
$$

We can find the least-squares solution ${\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\,A\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\end{aligned}
$$

Since the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\  & ={(\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \\ 2 & -1 \\ 0 & 1\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\,\,\,\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{bmatrix} \\  & ={\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\ -3 & 3\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{3} \\ -\frac{1}{3}\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ \frac{ {\mathbf{v}}_{1}}{\Vert  {\mathbf{v}}_{1} \Vert } & \frac{ {\mathbf{v}}_{2}}{\Vert  {\mathbf{v}}_{2} \Vert } & \frac{ {\mathbf{v}}_{3}}{\Vert  {\mathbf{v}}_{3} \Vert } \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{13}} \\ 0 & {r}_{22} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{23}} \\ 0 & 0 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{33}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}
$$

Consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\big\{ \mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3 \big\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $\Vert\mathbf{v}_1\Vert =2,$ $\Vert\mathbf{v}_2\Vert =3,$ $\Vert\mathbf{v}_3\Vert =4,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-3{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}.\end{aligned}\right.
$$

What is the value of $r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33}?$

First, we normalize the vectors $\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3 \mathbin{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\frac{{v}_{1}}{2}\,⟹\,{v}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\ {q}_{2} & =\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\,⟹\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\ {q}_{3} & =\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\frac{{v}_{3}}{4}\,⟹\,{v}_{3}=4{q}_{3}.\end{aligned}
$$

Now, the set $\big\{ \mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2,\mathbf{q}_3 \big\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.

Next, let's rewrite the vectors $\mathbf{a}_1, \mathbf{a}_2, \mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\mathbf{q}_1, \mathbf{q}_2, \mathbf{q}_3 \mathbin{:}$

$$
{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{v}}_{1} = {\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{v}}_{2} = {\mathbf{a}}_{2} - {\mathbf{v}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{v}}_{3} = {\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 3 {\mathbf{v}}_{1} + 2 {\mathbf{v}}_{2}\end{array} \right. }
	\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
	{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\mathbf{v}}_{1} = 2 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\mathbf{v}}_{1} + {\mathbf{v}}_{2} = 2 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + 3 {\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{3} = 3 {\mathbf{v}}_{1} - 2 {\mathbf{v}}_{2} + {\mathbf{v}}_{3} = 6 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} - 6 {\mathbf{q}}_{2} + 4 {\mathbf{q}}_{3}\end{array} \right. }
$$

The final system can be written in matrix form as follows:
$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}2 & 2 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{6} \\ 0 & 3 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 6} \\ 0 & 0 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{4}\end{array} \right]}.
	}^{\Large R}
$$

Therefore, 

$$
r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33} = 6+(-6)+4 = 4.
$$

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 2 \\ 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{q}_{11} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {q}_{12}} \\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\ 0 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{22}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

Consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\mathbf{a}_1,\mathbf{a}_2$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of $q_{12} \cdot r_{22}?$

Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct a matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize $\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\mathbf{a}_2\mathbf{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\ {q}_{2} & =\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Now, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\,A \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ -\frac{2}{3} & \frac{1}{3} & \frac{2}{3}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\ 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\sqrt{2} & 0 \\ 0 & 3\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A\mathbin{:}$

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 2 \\ 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 2\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}} & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}}} \\ 0 & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} \\ {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}} & {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}\sqrt{2} & 0 \\ 0 & \bbox[2pt,lightgray]{3}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}
$$

Finally, $q_{12} \cdot r_{22} = -\dfrac{2}{3} \cdot 3 = -2.$

The **QR factorization** of an $m\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns is the matrix product

$$
\underset{{\large m} \times {\large n}}{A}
=
\underset{{\large m} \times {\large n}}{Q} \times \underset{{\large n} \times {\large n}}{R},
$$

where 

- $Q$ is an $m \times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\text{Col}(A),$ and

- $R$ is an $n \times n$ upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its diagonal.

For example, the $3\times 2$ matrix

$$
A = {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}2 & - 3 \\ - 4 & 3 \\ 4 & 0\end{array} \right]}
$$

has the QR factorization

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}2 & - 3 \\ - 4 & 3 \\ 4 & 0\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} & - {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}} \\ - {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}} & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} \\ {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}} & {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}6 & - 3 \\ 0 & 3\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

Let's consider the following system of linear equations:

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-2 \\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=4 \\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\end{aligned}\right.
$$

It is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. 

Let's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\mathbf x = \mathbf b\mathbin{:}$
$$
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 1 \\ - 1 & - 4 \\ 2 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
}_{ A}
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
}_{ \mathbf{x}}
=
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 2 \\ 4 \\ 2\end{array} \right]}
}_{ \mathbf{b}}
$$

We are interested in vectors $\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathbf b$ is given by

$$
\Vert  A{\hat{\mathbf{x}}} - \mathbf{b} \Vert.
$$

The smaller the value of $\Vert  A{\hat{\mathbf{x}}} - \mathbf{b} \Vert,$ the better our approximation $\hat{\mathbf{x}}$ will be. 

In terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\mathbf{b}.$
Therefore, $A{\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$

![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)

It can be shown that ${\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by 

$$
\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\,A\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\end{aligned}
$$

We'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\hat{\mathbf{x}}} = \mathbf b$ by $A^T.$

Since the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \! A)^{-1},$ we obtain

$$
{\hat{\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \! A)^{-1}A^T \mathbf{b}.
$$

We now compute the right-hand side as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\  & ={(\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\ 1 & -4 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ -1 & -4 \\ 2 & -1\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\,\,\,\,\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\ 1 & -4 & -1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 4 \\ 2\end{bmatrix} \\  & ={\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\ 3 & 18\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ -20\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\frac{1}{99}\begin{bmatrix}18 & -3 \\ -3 & 6\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ -20\end{bmatrix} \\  & =\begin{bmatrix}\frac{8}{33} \\ -\frac{38}{33}\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

The vector ${\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.

**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \! A\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem.

Let's consider the following inconsistent system of equations:

$$
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 4 \\ - 2 & 8\end{array} \right]}
}_{ A}
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
}_{ \mathbf{x}}
=
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{c}1 \\ 3\end{array} \right]}
}_{ \mathbf{b}}
$$

Since the system is inconsistent, we cannot find an exact solution. However, as we've previously seen, we can attempt to find the so-called least-squares solution, which is a vector ${\hat{\mathbf{x}}}$ such that

$$
\|  A{\hat{\mathbf{x}}} - \mathbf{b} \|
$$

is as small as possible. 

As before, the least-squares solution can be found by solving the corresponding normal equation, given by

$$
\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\,A\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\end{aligned}
$$

Now, note the following: 

- Previously, we solved the normal equation by left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T A)^{-1}.$

- However, in this case, the second column of $A$ is a multiple of the first.

- So, the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent.

- Therefore, $A^T A$ is not invertible.

To solve the normal equation in this case, we proceed as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\,A\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b \\ \begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\ -4 & 8\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\ -2 & 8\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{bmatrix} & =\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\ -4 & 8\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{bmatrix} \\ \begin{bmatrix}5 & -20 \\ -20 & 80\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{bmatrix} & =\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\ 20\end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

Solving the last equation by Gaussian elimination, we obtain the general solution

$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 + 4 {x}_{2} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]} =
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]} + {\color{blue}x_2 {\left[ \begin{array}{c}4 \\ 1\end{array} \right]}},
\quad
x_2 \in \mathbb{R}.
$$

Notice that our solution is not unique! The second term of the least-squares solution (highlighted in blue) represents vectors from the null space of $A.$ 

In general, if a matrix $A$ has collinear columns, then the least-squares solution to $A\mathbf x = \mathbf b$ is not unique.

Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}
$$

Suppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  

$$
\{\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\ {q}_{2}=\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\ {q}_{3}=\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\end{aligned}
$$

How do we find $R?$
First, let's rewrite the vectors $\mathbf{a}_1, \mathbf{a}_2, \mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\mathbf{q}_1, \mathbf{q}_2, \mathbf{q}_3 \mathbin{:}$
$$
{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{2}} {\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} {\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} {\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\mathbf{q}}_{2}\end{array} \right. }
	\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
	{\left\{  \begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\color{red}2} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\color{red}- 9} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\color{blue}3} {\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\color{red}8} {\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\color{blue}4} {\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\color{purple}4} {\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\end{array} \right. }
$$

In this new system, the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\ {\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\ | & | & |\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{red}2} & {\color{red}- 9} & {\color{red}8} \\ 0 & {\color{blue}3} & {\color{blue}- 4} \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{purple}4}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

To see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$

$$
\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\ \overbrace{[\begin{aligned}-\,\,\,\, & {a}_{1} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {a}_{2} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {a}_{3} & \,\,\,\,-\end{aligned}]}^{{A}^{T}} & =\overbrace{[\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\ 8 & -4 & 4\end{aligned}]}^{{R}^{T}}\overbrace{[\begin{aligned}-\,\,\,\, & {q}_{1} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {q}_{2} & \,\,\,\,- \\ -\,\,\,\, & {q}_{3} & \,\,\,\,-\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\end{aligned}
$$

If we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations.

Let's now go through the QR factorization algorithm to find the factorization of the matrix

$$
A = 
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}2 & 1 \\ 4 & 3 \\ - 4 & - 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

**Step 1**. Orthogonalize the columns of $A$ to get the columns of $Q.$ Using the Gram-Schmidt process, we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ -2\end{bmatrix},\,{q}_{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

**Step 2**. Compute $R=Q^TA.$ We get

$$
\begin{aligned}R=\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\ 0 & \sqrt{2}\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the QR factorization of $A$ is

$$
\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}2 & 1 \\ 4 & 3 \\ - 4 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large A}
	=
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}} & 0 \\ {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}} & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}} \\ - {\displaystyle \frac{2}{3}} & {\displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large Q}
	\overbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}6 & 3 \\ 0 & \sqrt{2}\end{array} \right]}
	}^{\Large R}.
$$

