1 + \cot^2 x= \csc^2{x}.
$$

Using Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity

$$
\coth^2x - 1=\csch^2{x}.
$$

Using the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
		\int_{\ln 3}^{\ln 6} \dfrac{\csch{x}}{\coth^2{x} - 1} \, \textrm{d}x
			&= \int_{\ln 3}^{\ln 6} \dfrac{\csch{x}}{\csch^2{x}} \, \textrm{d}x \\[5pt]
			&= \int_{\ln 3}^{\ln 6} \sinh{x} \, \textrm{d}x \\[5pt]
			&= \cosh{x} \Big|_{\ln 3}^{\ln 6}\\[5pt]
			&= \cosh(\ln 6) - \cosh(\ln 3)\\[5pt]
			&= \left(\dfrac{e^{\ln 6}+e^{-\ln 6}}{2}\right) - \left(\dfrac{e^{\ln 3}+e^{-\ln 3}}{2}\right)\\[5pt]
			&= \left(\dfrac{6 + \dfrac{1}{6}}{2}\right) - \left(\dfrac{3 + \dfrac{1}{3}}{2}\right)\\[5pt]
			&= \dfrac{37}{12} - \dfrac{5}{3}\\[5pt]
			&= \dfrac{37}{12} - \dfrac{20}{12} \\[5pt]
            &= \dfrac{17}{12}
	\end{aligned}
$$

First, recall that

$$
\dfrac{9}{10}
$$

can be viewed as the sum of $9$ instances of the fraction

$$
\dfrac{1}{10}{:}
$$

$$
\overbrace{
	\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} 
}^{\large { {9} \textrm{ times}}} = \dfrac{9}{10}
$$

Examining the known fractions on the left-hand side of the given equation, from left to right, we have the following:

- $2$ instances of $\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\color{blue}\dfrac{2}{10}$
- $4$ instances of $\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\color{red}\dfrac{4}{10}$

Let's add these to our diagram:

$$
\overbrace{
	\underbrace{\color{blue}\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10}}_{\color{blue}\dfrac{2}{10}} + \underbrace{\color{red}\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\color{red}\dfrac{4}{10}} +
	\color{violet}\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10}  
}^{\large { {9} \textrm{ times}}} = \dfrac{9}{10}
$$

We have $3$ instances of

$$
\dfrac1{10}
$$

remaining, and these give the fraction

$$
{\color{violet}{\dfrac3{10}}}
$$

$$
\overbrace{
	\underbrace{\color{blue}\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10} }_{\color{blue}\dfrac{2}{10}} + 
	\underbrace{\color{red}\dfrac{1}{10} + \dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\color{red}\dfrac{4}{10}} + 
	\underbrace{\color{violet}\dfrac{1}{10}+\dfrac{1}{10}+ \dfrac{1}{10}}_{\color{violet}\dfrac{3}{10}}
}^{\large { {9} \textrm{ times}}} = \dfrac{9}{10}
$$

This gives the decomposition

$$
{\color{blue}\dfrac{2}{10}} + {\color{red}\dfrac{4}{10}}+ \bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\color{violet}\dfrac{3}{10}} = \dfrac{9}{10}
$$

Therefore, the missing fraction is

$$
{\color{violet}{\dfrac3{10}}}
$$

